# Wolseley 10/12 hp open two-seater, 1901-1906.
#
# The horizontal-twin Wolseley: 2,606 cc, 1,051 built from 1900 to 1906.
# Wolseley was Britain's largest car maker by 1913 with 3,000 cars.
# The period catalogued the car as a 10 hp; later sources call it a 12 hp,
# and the pack name records both.
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wolseley_Motors
#
# Dimensions 3.4 x 1.7 x 1.7 m, wheelbase 2.3 m. All four are estimates
# measured from the photographs; no published dimension box was found.
#
# Shape reference: the most side-on image found is a front three-quarter
# about 20 degrees forward of profile, both wheels visible. The flank and
# tail are estimated from it and the photographs' proportions. No rear view
# of AT 45 exists in Commons. The limitation is recorded.
#
# Colours: grade (d), no period UK document consulted. Black, Dark Green,
# Maroon and Grey are plain colour names. Weights 2, 2, 2, 1, total 7,
# provisional pending the central rebase.
#
# Speed 50 km/h. Comfort 18/357, on the line 330 + 1.5 x 18. Class 3.
# Dates 1901-1906 production, road tail staggered 1914-1916.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/wolseley-10-12.blend. Interchange:
# citycars/4.5/wolseley-10-12.dae. References:
# citycars/references/wolseley-10-12/ (gitignored; Wikimedia Commons).
#
# Accepted deviations: no true side elevation exists; the best is a front
# three-quarter about 20 degrees forward of profile, and no rear view of AT 45
# exists. The flank and tail are estimates from the photographs and their
# proportions. The four-seat tonneau body is modelled.


